# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reading the result
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Readable at 18 seconds remaining. Empty at 2 seconds past. No grace period,
# no lag: the boundary is where the registry says it is.
#
# Two details make this evidence rather than a self-test.
#
# It reads through the Universal Resolver, the path any client takes. Reading
# the resolver directly would have kept answering — the record is still in its
# storage, untouched. Expiry does not delete anything; it ends resolution, and
# resolution is how a client finds a record at all.
#
# The expiry was verified against the registry (findExpiry) rather than assumed
# from what we passed in. Those agreed, so the countdown above is measured
# against the contract's own view of the deadline.
#
# What this means for NextKey: "limit access to seven days" is not a policy we
# apply. It is a uint64 the registry enforces against everyone, including us.
# The ciphertext and every wrapped key survive in resolver storage exactly as
# before — and become unreachable by name, which is the only way anyone was
# ever reaching them.
#
# fleeting23418.nextkey.eth is left expired on purpose. It is the artifact.
